package Patterns;

import java.util.Scanner;

public class Q22 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.print("Please enter no: ");
        int n = sc.nextInt();

        for(int i = 0 ; i<2 * n -1; i++){
            for (int j = 0; j < 2 * n - 1; j++) {

                int top = i;
                int left = j ;
                int bottom = (2*n-2)-i;
                int right = (2*n-2)-j;
                int value = Math.min(Math.min(top,bottom), Math.min(left,right));

                System.out.print(n-value+" ");


            }
            System.out.println();
        }
    }
}


is symmetric around the center.

Layer Representation
Layer 0 → n
Layer 1 → n - 1
Layer 2 → n - 2
...
Center  → 1

Therefore, the main task is:

Determine the layer of each cell.

Grid Size

The numbers decrease from n to 1 and then increase back to n.

n → n-1 → n-2 → ... → 1 → ... → n-2 → n-1 → n

Hence, the total number of rows and columns is:

(n - 1) + 1 + (n - 1)
= 2n - 1

So the pattern size is:

(2 * n - 1) × (2 * n - 1)
Distance from Borders

For every cell (i, j), calculate its distance from all four borders.

int top = i;
int left = j;
int bottom = (2 * n - 2) - i;
int right = (2 * n - 2) - j;
Why?
top = i
Distance from the top border.
left = j
Distance from the left border.
bottom = (2*n-2) - i
Distance from the bottom border.
2*n-2 is the last row index.
right = (2*n-2) - j
Distance from the right border.
2*n-2 is the last column index.
Finding the Layer

A cell belongs to the layer of its nearest border.

Therefore:

int layer = Math.min(
                Math.min(top, bottom),
                Math.min(left, right)
            );

or

layer = min(top, left, bottom, right);
Example

For cell (2,3) when n = 4:

top = 2
left = 3
bottom = 4
right = 3

Nearest border:

min(2,3,4,3) = 2

So the cell belongs to Layer 2.

Calculating the Value

The outermost layer contains n.

Every inner layer decreases by 1.

Therefore:

value = n - layer;
Example
Layer 0 → 4
Layer 1 → 3
Layer 2 → 2
Layer 3 → 1
Algorithm
Create a grid of size (2*n - 1) × (2*n - 1).
For every cell (i, j):
Compute distances from top, left, bottom, and right borders.
Find the minimum distance.
This minimum distance represents the layer number.
Print n - layer.
Move to the next row after completing each column traversal.
Time Complexity

The grid contains:

(2n - 1) × (2n - 1)

cells.

Each cell is processed exactly once.

Time Complexity: O((2n - 1)²)
               = O(n²)
Space Complexity

No extra data structure is used.

Space Complexity: O(1)
Pattern Solving Trick

Whenever you see a pattern made of concentric squares/rings, think:

Outer Layer → Largest Value
Inner Layers → Gradually Smaller Values
